referenceSolutions.python:
def floorRegisterFloorTicket(root):
    a=root
    out=[]
    from collections import deque
    queue=deque([a] if a else [])
    while queue:
        s=0
        for _ in range(len(queue)):
            node=queue.popleft()
            s+=node.val
            if node.left: queue.append(node.left)
            if node.right: queue.append(node.right)
        out.append(s)
    return min(out,default=0)

referenceSolutions.java:
class Solution {
  public int floorRegisterFloorTicket(TreeNode root) {
    TreeNode a=root; List<Integer> out=new ArrayList<>();
    {if(a!=null){Deque<TreeNode>queue=new ArrayDeque<>();queue.add(a);while(!queue.isEmpty()){int count=queue.size(),s=0;while(count-->0){TreeNode node=queue.remove();s+=node.val;if(node.left!=null)queue.add(node.left);if(node.right!=null)queue.add(node.right);}out.add(s);}}}
    int answer=out.isEmpty()?0:out.get(0);for(int x:out)answer=Math.min(answer,x);return answer;
  }
}

privateData.editorialMd:
Build levelSums

`root` is the top node of a binary tree. Each node has a value and up to two children. The `root` is at depth 0, its children are at depth 1, and so on. Add the values at each depth. Put one sum per depth in `levelSums`, starting at depth 0. Missing children add nothing. An empty tree gives an empty `levelSums`.

Return the answer

Find the smallest number in `levelSums`. Store it in `smallest`. Complete `floorRegisterFloorTicket` so it returns `smallest`. Return it, do not print it.

Why this works

Each value in `levelSums` follows task 1. The later tasks use only those values. Keeping these steps separate helps avoid returning the list when the question asks for one number. Check empty input, one item, repeated values, and the largest allowed input.

Efficiency goal: time O(n); extra memory, including the helper list, O(n). n is the number of nodes. These labels describe how work and memory grow with the input.

